# Serving the page a seed lands on.
#
# `/s` is the path every seed link already points at, and the path the
# association file claims. It is a file with no extension for the same reason
# the association file is: the path is the format, and a path with `.html` on
# the end would be a different path from the one already minted into links and
# registered as an App Clip prefix.
#
# A directory called `s` with an `index.html` in it would serve the same page,
# and would cost a redirect from `/s` to `/s/` on every arrival. A fragment
# survives a redirect, but a redirect on the one path this whole site exists to
# answer is a moving part that buys nothing.
#
# `g` and `t` are here for the same reason and were not, which is a bug this
# file carried from the moment the garden was added: with only `s` named, `/g`
# arrived as `application/octet-stream` and a browser downloaded it instead of
# drawing it. Nothing said so — the request is a 200 and the log is clean — and
# it was found by serving the directory locally the way a host serves it. Any
# page added here has to be added below, and `tools/site/serve.py` keeps the
# same list so that what is looked at locally is what is served.
<FilesMatch "^(s|g|t)$">
    ForceType text/html
</FilesMatch>

AddDefaultCharset utf-8

# No redirects on the seed path. Not to a trailing slash, not to `www`, not to
# anything: this is the URL in the links people have already sent.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteRule ^s$ - [L]
</IfModule>

# The passage banks and the language manifest are generated by
# `tools/site/export.py` and served as they are written.
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    AddType application/json .json
</IfModule>

# What this file cannot do, said here rather than assumed:
#
# **Request logging.** docs/WEBSITE.md asks for no request log on `/s`, or the
# shortest one the host permits. That is a setting in the 20i control panel
# rather than a directive here, so it is a deploy step — see Server/README.md.
# Nothing on this page should claim more than what is actually switched off.
#
# **Robots.** No directive here, deliberately. `noindex` is settled for shared
# plant pages, which publish somebody's name beside a line about a real
# meeting. This page publishes nothing: with a seed in it the content is in the
# fragment, which a crawler never receives, and with no seed it is the page
# that says what Peace Garden is. Whether the site is ever *meant* to be found
# in a search engine is an open question in docs/WEBSITE.md, and it is a
# question about a marketing page rather than about seeds.
